  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>About a ten-minute walk from a small car park on the road to the Col de Turini, the Cascade du Piaon sits in a wooded valley above Sospel, with a pool whose jade-green reflections invite a swim in summer.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>From Sospel, take the D2566 heading north for about 5 km (past the campsite), and park at one of the two roadside spots. A fairly steep path, over about 200 m of elevation change, then leads down to the foot of the waterfall. In summer, the pool becomes a genuine natural swimming hole, with jumps possible from the surrounding rocks — an activity that obviously doesn't concern dogs, but gives an idea of how busy the site gets in fine weather. Two independent, matching sources (cascadesdefrance.fr and spot-de-baignade.fr) state that the site sits on private land with restricted access without the owner's permission — but no official source (Sospel town hall or tourist office) could be identified to confirm or deny this status: best to check this point with the tourist office before heading there. The path is also described by several independent sources as "very steep" and "slippery especially in wet weather," with some vertiginous sections — no accident or drowning has been documented for this specific site, however (which doesn't guarantee there's no risk, only that none has been recorded in our sources). No official rule about dogs was found for this site either.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: the access path, short but steep, can get slippery for a dog after rain — bring a short leash for the descent.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The village of Sospel, with its old bridge and market, pairs well with an outing to the Cascade du Piaon; the road to the Col de Turini then continues toward the Vésubie valley.</p>
